\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794559705017,"end":1794559706892}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794559706895,"sessionID":"ses_e2be9abf0ffea6S3OJ4PZfZtpb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d41802480014fvHLNtYsDVuLm","sessionID":"ses_e2be9abf0ffea6S3OJ4PZfZtpb","messageID":"msg_1d417f403001E0xmq4j4fUCfLS","type":"tool","id":"call_f90886eac66b4bb5a4109b17","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md, lines 1-162\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Root cause and debugging\"\n3: description: \"Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"fix the producer not the symptom\"\n6:   - \"name the mechanism\"\n7:   - \"symptom fix smells\"\n8:   - \"special case\"\n9:   - \"add a retry\"\n10:   - \"add a sleep\"\n11:   - \"broadened catch\"\n12:   - \"loosen the assertion\"\n13:   - \"never weaken a check\"\n14:   - \"make the red green\"\n15:   - \"it's a flake\"\n16:   - \"infra problem\"\n17:   - \"pre-existing failure\"\n18:   - \"not caused by my change\"\n19:   - \"second attempt at the same fix\"\n20:   - \"level up to the seam\"\n21:   - \"stuck escalation format\"\n22:   - \"flaky test\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.2\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Root cause and debugging\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when something is red.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Anything fails: a test, a build, a check, a runtime error, a wrong output.\n36: - You are on the second attempt at the same fix.\n37: - About to add a special case, a retry, a sleep, a broadened `catch`, or a loosened assertion.\n38: - You are tempted to call a failure a flake, an infra problem, or pre-existing.\n39: \n40: ## The rule\n41: \n42: **Fix the producer, not the symptom. Every fix names the mechanism that caused the\n43: failure.**\n44: \n45: If you cannot say *why* it broke in one sentence, you have not found the cause, you\n46: have found a place where the symptom stops appearing.\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. THE LOOP\n51: \n52: 1. **Reproduce the exact symptom** safely. Not a similar one, the same failure, the\n53:    same message.\n54: 2. **Read the whole error.** The top line names where it surfaced; the cause is usually\n55:    the innermost frame in code you control, and the message is usually literal.\n56: 3. **Locate the producer:** the code that created the bad value or state, not the code\n57:    that choked on it.\n58: 4. **Trace to the consumers.** Anything else reading that producer has the same bug,\n59:    surfaced or not.\n60: 5. **Fix at the source.**\n61: 6. **Re-run the same check that failed**, then the whole gate.\n62: \n63: Step 1 is not optional. If you cannot reproduce it you cannot prove you fixed it, and\n64: you should say so instead of claiming a fix (see the negative control in\n65: `evidence-and-proof.md`).\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. SYMPTOM-FIX SMELLS\n70: \n71: Each is evidence you are patching in the wrong place:\n72: \n73: | Smell | What it is really telling you |\n74: |-------|-------------------------------|\n75: | A special case for one caller | The seam between the module and that caller is wrong |\n76: | A retry around a deterministic operation | You do not know why it fails, and now it fails five times |\n77: | A `sleep` | A real ordering or lifecycle bug, now timing-dependent |\n78: | A broadened `catch` or bare `except` | The failure is now silent and the wrong answer ships |\n79: | A widened type or an added `any` | The type was right; the value is wrong |\n80: | Changing the expected value to match the actual | You just wrote the bug down as the specification |\n81: | A default that papers over a missing value | The producer never set it, and now nobody will find out |\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. WHEN AN ATTEMPT REPEATS\n86: \n87: **If an attempt repeats without producing new evidence, stop patching at the failure\n88: site.** The local retry count is set outside this file. This stop fires on repetition\n89: without new evidence, not on a count.\n90: \n91: Do not repeat the same guess with a variation. Instead:\n92: \n93: 1. **Restate the problem one level up:** at the interface, the data flow, or the module\n94:    boundary rather than the line.\n95: 2. **Inspect the interface that actually exists.** Read the signature, type, schema,\n96:    docs, caller.\n97: 3. **Try once from the new framing.**\n98: \n99: Verify commands, flags, paths and APIs exist before relying on them. When an option turns\n100: out unsupported, read the available interface and change approach, do not re-run the\n101: same command with a different spelling.\n102: \n103: **Naming the seam.** When a fix only works by special-casing a caller, the boundary is\n104: wrong. Say so: **name the seam and the files a seam fix would touch**, then ask.\n105: `scope-discipline.md` still binds, \"the real fix is over there\" does not grant a yes.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 4. NEVER MAKE A CHECK PASS BY WEAKENING IT\n110: \n111: Not a fix, in any circumstance: skipping, disabling, quarantining or deleting a failing\n112: test; loosening an assertion until it passes; excluding the failing file from the linter,\n113: type checker or matcher; committing an empty change or re-triggering a run for a\n114: different roll.\n115: \n116: If a test is genuinely wrong, that is a finding to raise with evidence, not a line to\n117: edit on your way past.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 5. \"FLAKE\" IS A CONCLUSION, NOT A STARTING HYPOTHESIS\n122: \n123: Only two things support it: the same check passed on this exact input before, or the run\n124: died before any test body executed (checkout, install, runner loss). Absent one of those,\n125: treat the failure as real. One re-run to confirm, at most; a second failure is a bug.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 6. OWNERSHIP\n130: \n131: \"Pre-existing.\" \"Not caused by my changes.\" \"Environment issue.\" These may be true and\n132: are never a stopping point. Diagnose far enough to know which it is, say what you found,\n133: then decide with the operator. A failure you walked past is a failure you shipped.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 7. WHEN YOU ARE STUCK, ESCALATION FORMAT\n138: \n139: Once repetition has replaced evidence and the retry budget is spent, escalate once:\n140: \n141: 1. **Symptom:** the exact error, verbatim.\n142: 2. **What I tried:** each attempt and the evidence it produced, not just what it was.\n143: 3. **What that rules out:** the hypotheses now eliminated.\n144: 4. **Options:** two or three, with the trade-off of each.\n145: 5. **Recommendation:** one, and why.\n146: \n147: A stuck report without evidence per attempt asks someone else to start from zero.\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n152: \n153: - [ ] Reproduced the exact symptom before changing anything.\n154: - [ ] I can state the mechanism in one sentence.\n155: - [ ] The fix is at the producer, not where the symptom surfaced, and I checked that\n156:       producer's other consumers.\n157: - [ ] No test, assertion, or check was weakened to get green.\n158: - [ ] If I called it a flake, I have one of the two pieces of evidence.\n159: - [ ] Re-ran the failing check *and* the whole gate.\n160: - [ ] When an attempt repeated, I restated the problem one level up instead of trying the same guess.\n161: - [ ] A fix that only works by special-casing a caller was reported as a seam, with the files a seam fix would touch, and asked about.\n162: - [ ] I owned the failure I met, and when stuck I escalated in the format with the evidence, the cause if known and the decision needed.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md, lines 1-162\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Root cause and debugging\"\n3: description: \"Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"fix the producer not the symptom\"\n6:   - \"name the mechanism\"\n7:   - \"symptom fix smells\"\n8:   - \"special case\"\n9:   - \"add a retry\"\n10:   - \"add a sleep\"\n11:   - \"broadened catch\"\n12:   - \"loosen the assertion\"\n13:   - \"never weaken a check\"\n14:   - \"make the red green\"\n15:   - \"it's a flake\"\n16:   - \"infra problem\"\n17:   - \"pre-existing failure\"\n18:   - \"not caused by my change\"\n19:   - \"second attempt at the same fix\"\n20:   - \"level up to the seam\"\n21:   - \"stuck escalation format\"\n22:   - \"flaky test\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.2\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Root cause and debugging\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when something is red.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Anything fails: a test, a build, a check, a runtime error, a wrong output.\n36: - You are on the second attempt at the same fix.\n37: - About to add a special case, a retry, a sleep, a broadened `catch`, or a loosened assertion.\n38: - You are tempted to call a failure a flake, an infra problem, or pre-existing.\n39: \n40: ## The rule\n41: \n42: **Fix the producer, not the symptom. Every fix names the mechanism that caused the\n43: failure.**\n44: \n45: If you cannot say *why* it broke in one sentence, you have not found the cause, you\n46: have found a place where the symptom stops appearing.\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. THE LOOP\n51: \n52: 1. **Reproduce the exact symptom** safely. Not a similar one, the same failure, the\n53:    same message.\n54: 2. **Read the whole error.** The top line names where it surfaced; the cause is usually\n55:    the innermost frame in code you control, and the message is usually literal.\n56: 3. **Locate the producer:** the code that created the bad value or state, not the code\n57:    that choked on it.\n58: 4. **Trace to the consumers.** Anything else reading that producer has the same bug,\n59:    surfaced or not.\n60: 5. **Fix at the source.**\n61: 6. **Re-run the same check that failed**, then the whole gate.\n62: \n63: Step 1 is not optional. If you cannot reproduce it you cannot prove you fixed it, and\n64: you should say so instead of claiming a fix (see the negative control in\n65: `evidence-and-proof.md`).\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. SYMPTOM-FIX SMELLS\n70: \n71: Each is evidence you are patching in the wrong place:\n72: \n73: | Smell | What it is really telling you |\n74: |-------|-------------------------------|\n75: | A special case for one caller | The seam between the module and that caller is wrong |\n76: | A retry around a deterministic operation | You do not know why it fails, and now it fails five times |\n77: | A `sleep` | A real ordering or lifecycle bug, now timing-dependent |\n78: | A broadened `catch` or bare `except` | The failure is now silent and the wrong answer ships |\n79: | A widened type or an added `any` | The type was right; the value is wrong |\n80: | Changing the expected value to match the actual | You just wrote the bug down as the specification |\n81: | A default that papers over a missing value | The producer never set it, and now nobody will find out |\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. WHEN AN ATTEMPT REPEATS\n86: \n87: **If an attempt repeats without producing new evidence, stop patching at the failure\n88: site.** The local retry count is set outside this file. This stop fires on repetition\n89: without new evidence, not on a count.\n90: \n91: Do not repeat the same guess with a variation. Instead:\n92: \n93: 1. **Restate the problem one level up:** at the interface, the data flow, or the module\n94:    boundary rather than the line.\n95: 2. **Inspect the interface that actually exists.** Read the signature, type, schema,\n96:    docs, caller.\n97: 3. **Try once from the new framing.**\n98: \n99: Verify commands, flags, paths and APIs exist before relying on them. When an option turns\n100: out unsupported, read the available interface and change approach, do not re-run the\n101: same command with a different spelling.\n102: \n103: **Naming the seam.** When a fix only works by special-casing a caller, the boundary is\n104: wrong. Say so: **name the seam and the files a seam fix would touch**, then ask.\n105: `scope-discipline.md` still binds, \"the real fix is over there\" does not grant a yes.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 4. NEVER MAKE A CHECK PASS BY WEAKENING IT\n110: \n111: Not a fix, in any circumstance: skipping, disabling, quarantining or deleting a failing\n112: test; loosening an assertion until it passes; excluding the failing file from the linter,\n113: type checker or matcher; committing an empty change or re-triggering a run for a\n114: different roll.\n115: \n116: If a test is genuinely wrong, that is a finding to raise with evidence, not a line to\n117: edit on your way past.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 5. \"FLAKE\" IS A CONCLUSION, NOT A STARTING HYPOTHESIS\n122: \n123: Only two things support it: the same check passed on this exact input before, or the run\n124: died before any test body executed (checkout, install, runner loss). Absent one of those,\n125: treat the failure as real. One re-run to confirm, at most; a second failure is a bug.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 6. OWNERSHIP\n130: \n131: \"Pre-existing.\" \"Not caused by my changes.\" \"Environment issue.\" These may be true and\n132: are never a stopping point. Diagnose far enough to know which it is, say what you found,\n133: then decide with the operator. A failure you walked past is a failure you shipped.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 7. WHEN YOU ARE STUCK, ESCALATION FORMAT\n138: \n139: Once repetition has replaced evidence and the retry budget is spent, escalate once:\n140: \n141: 1. **Symptom:** the exact error, verbatim.\n142: 2. **What I tried:** each attempt and the evidence it produced, not just what it was.\n143: 3. **What that rules out:** the hypotheses now eliminated.\n144: 4. **Options:** two or three, with the trade-off of each.\n145: 5. **Recommendation:** one, and why.\n146: \n147: A stuck report without evidence per attempt asks someone else to start from zero.\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n152: \n153: - [ ] Reproduced the exact symptom before changing anything.\n154: - [ ] I can state the mechanism in one sentence.\n155: - [ ] The fix is at the producer, not where the symptom surfaced, and I checked that\n156:       producer's other consumers.\n157: - [ ] No test, assertion, or check was weakened to get green.\n158: - [ ] If I called it a flake, I have one of the two pieces of evidence.\n159: - [ ] Re-ran the failing check *and* the whole gate.\n160: - [ ] When an attempt repeated, I restated the problem one level up instead of trying the same guess.\n161: - [ ] A fix that only works by special-casing a caller was reported as a seam, with the files a seam fix would touch, and asked about.\n162: - [ ] I owned the failure I met, and when stuck I escalated in the format with the evidence, the cause if known and the decision needed."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794559705672,"end":1794559706895}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794559706900,"sessionID":"ses_e2be9abf0ffea6S3OJ4PZfZtpb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d418055f001wQPL3Dij9lFn4M","sessionID":"ses_e2be9abf0ffea6S3OJ4PZfZtpb","messageID":"msg_1d417f403001E0xmq4j4fUCfLS","type":"tool","id":"call_1b124ee2fb7f4e7489aacb92","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md, lines 1-301\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Quality Standards\n3: description: Module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns for JavaScript files.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript quality standards\"\n6:   - \"javascript module organization\"\n7:   - \"commonjs export pattern\"\n8:   - \"javascript error handling standards\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.3.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Quality Standards\n15: \n16: Reach for this file when a JavaScript module boundary, error path or security rule needs a decision that survives review.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Establishes module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns that all JavaScript code must follow.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing new JavaScript modules\n29: - Reviewing code for quality compliance\n30: - Setting up error handling and security patterns\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. MODULE ORGANIZATION\n35: \n36: ### Module-System Boundary\n37: \n38: Use the module format required by the file's package boundary and loader\n39: contract. Do not force one JavaScript module style across all paths.\n40: \n41: | Surface | Module Pattern | Notes |\n42: |---------|----------------|-------|\n43: | `.js` files | Follow the nearest package `type` and loader contract | `\"type\": \"module\"` selects ESM; otherwise use the package's CommonJS boundary |\n44: | `.cjs` files | CommonJS | The extension selects CommonJS |\n45: | `.mjs` files | ESM | The verifier skips `'use strict'` enforcement for `.mjs` |\n46: | `.opencode/plugins/*.{js,mjs,ts}` | ESM default export | Required by the OpenCode plugin loader |\n47: \n48: ### CommonJS Pattern\n49: \n50: - Use CommonJS for `.cjs` files and `.js` files whose package boundary selects CommonJS.\n51: - Include the repository's required `'use strict'` directive in those files.\n52: - ESM `.js` files follow their package boundary and also keep that directive to satisfy the repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM.\n53: \n54: ```javascript\n55: 'use strict';\n56: \n57: // ... imports and implementation ...\n58: \n59: module.exports = {\n60:   // Exports here\n61: };\n62: ```\n63: \n64: **Evidence**: Current TypeScript packages are ESM/NodeNext; this CommonJS\n65: pattern applies to legacy `.js/.cjs` utility surfaces and test runners that use\n66: `require`.\n67: \n68: ### Export Pattern\n69: \n70: - Export functions using `camelCase` names.\n71: - For MCP handlers, include `snake_case` aliases for backward compatibility.\n72: - Do not apply this export shape to OpenCode plugin paths; those use `export default`.\n73: \n74: ```javascript\n75: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n76: // 5. EXPORTS\n77: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n78: \n79: module.exports = {\n80:   // Primary exports (camelCase)\n81:   loadConfig,\n82:   triggerLookup,\n83:   validateInput,\n84: \n85:   // Backward-compatible aliases (snake_case) — MCP handlers only\n86:   load_config: loadConfig,\n87:   trigger_lookup: triggerLookup,\n88:   validate_input: validateInput\n89: };\n90: ```\n91: \n92: **Evidence**: Legacy CommonJS export examples are historical; current\n93: system-spec-kit TypeScript source uses ESM `export` statements and compiles per\n94: workspace package settings.\n95: \n96: ### Barrel Exports\n97: \n98: Index files re-export from multiple modules.\n99: \n100: ```javascript\n101: // index.js\n102: 'use strict';\n103: \n104: module.exports = {\n105:   ...require('./config'),\n106:   ...require('./database'),\n107:   ...require('./search')\n108: };\n109: ```\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 3. ERROR HANDLING\n114: \n115: ### Guard Clause Pattern\n116: \n117: Validate inputs at function start. Return early on failure.\n118: \n119: ```javascript\n120: function processData(input, options) {\n121:   // Guard clauses first\n122:   if (!input || typeof input !== 'string') {\n123:     return { success: false, error: 'Invalid input: expected string' };\n124:   }\n125: \n126:   if (!options || typeof options !== 'object') {\n127:     return { success: false, error: 'Invalid options: expected object' };\n128:   }\n129: \n130:   // Main logic after guards pass\n131:   const result = transform(input, options);\n132:   return { success: true, data: result };\n133: }\n134: ```\n135: \n136: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts`\n137: \n138: ### Try-Catch Pattern\n139: \n140: Wrap async operations with specific error handling.\n141: \n142: ```javascript\n143: async function fetchData(query) {\n144:   try {\n145:     const result = await database.query(query);\n146:     return { success: true, data: result };\n147:   } catch (error) {\n148:     logger.error(`[fetchData] Query failed: ${error.message}`);\n149:     return { success: false, error: error.message };\n150:   }\n151: }\n152: ```\n153: \n154: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts`\n155: \n156: ### Custom Error Classes\n157: \n158: Extend Error for domain-specific errors.\n159: \n160: ```javascript\n161: /**\n162:  * Custom error for spec-folder operations.\n163:  */\n164: class SpecFolderError extends Error {\n165:   /**\n166:    * Create a SpecFolderError.\n167:    * @param {string} code - Error code (e.g., 'SPEC_FOLDER_OUTSIDE_ROOT')\n168:    * @param {string} message - Error description\n169:    * @param {Object} [details] - Additional details\n170:    */\n171:   constructor(code, message, details = {}) {\n172:     super(message);\n173:     this.name = 'SpecFolderError';\n174:     this.code = code;\n175:     this.details = details;\n176:     this.recoveryHint = undefined;\n177:     Object.setPrototypeOf(this, SpecFolderError.prototype);\n178:   }\n179: }\n180: \n181: module.exports = { SpecFolderError };\n182: ```\n183: \n184: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts`\n185: \n186: ### Error Response Pattern\n187: \n188: Consistent error response structure.\n189: \n190: ```javascript\n191: // Success response\n192: { success: true, data: result }\n193: \n194: // Error response\n195: { success: false, error: 'Error message', code: 'ERROR_CODE' }\n196: ```\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 4. CONSOLE LOGGING\n201: \n202: ### Bracketed Module Prefix\n203: \n204: All console output uses bracketed module identifier.\n205: \n206: ```javascript\n207: // Format: [module-name] Message\n208: console.log(`[transaction-manager] Commit completed in ${elapsedMs}ms`);\n209: console.error(`[folder-discovery] Description merge failed: ${error.message}`);\n210: console.warn(`[config] Using default value for missing key: ${key}`);\n211: ```\n212: \n213: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/storage/transaction-manager.ts`\n214: \n215: ### Log Levels\n216: \n217: | Level   | Usage                            | Example                                    |\n218: |---------|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n219: | `log`   | General information              | `[server] Request received`                |\n220: | `error` | Errors requiring attention       | `[db] Connection failed: timeout`          |\n221: | `warn`  | Potential issues, fallbacks      | `[config] Missing value, using default`    |\n222: | `debug` | Development-only verbose output  | `[search] Query plan: ${JSON.stringify()}` |\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: ## 5. JSDOC DOCUMENTATION\n227: \n228: ### Function Documentation\n229: \n230: Required for all exported functions.\n231: \n232: ```javascript\n233: /**\n234:  * Look up trigger-index entries matching a query.\n235:  *\n236:  * @param {string} query - Search query text\n237:  * @param {Object} options - Lookup configuration\n238:  * @param {number} [options.limit=10] - Maximum results to return\n239:  * @param {string} [options.specFolder] - Filter by spec folder path\n240:  * @param {string[]} [options.anchors] - Filter by anchor types\n241:  * @returns {Promise<Array<Object>>} Array of matching trigger entries\n242:  * @throws {SpecFolderError} If the spec folder cannot be resolved\n243:  *\n244:  * @example\n245:  * const results = await lookupTriggers('authentication', {\n246:  *   limit: 5,\n247:  *   specFolder: 'specs/007-auth'\n248:  * });\n249:  */\n250: async function lookupTriggers(query, options = {}) {\n251:   // implementation\n252: }\n253: ```\n254: \n255: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/lookup-trigger-index.mjs`\n256: \n257: ### Type Annotations\n258: \n259: Common JSDoc type patterns.\n260: \n261: ```javascript\n262: /**\n263:  * @param {string} name - Required string parameter\n264:  * @param {number} [count=10] - Optional with default\n265:  * @param {Object} config - Object parameter\n266:  * @param {string} config.path - Required property\n267:  * @param {boolean} [config.verbose] - Optional property\n268:  * @param {string|null} value - Union type\n269:  * @param {Array<string>} items - Typed array\n270:  * @param {Promise<Object>} result - Promise type\n271:  * @param {Function} callback - Function type\n272:  */\n273: ```\n274: \n275: ### Class Documentation\n276: \n277: ```javascript\n278: /**\n279:  * Manages vector-based semantic search index.\n280:  *\n281:  * @class\n282:  * @example\n283:  * const index = new VectorIndex(dbPath);\n284:  * await index.initialize();\n285:  */\n286: class VectorIndex {\n287:   /**\n288:    * Create a VectorIndex instance.\n289:    * @param {string} dbPath - Path to SQLite database\n290:    */\n291:   constructor(dbPath) {\n292:     this.dbPath = dbPath;\n293:   }\n294: }\n295: ```\n296: \n297: ---\n298: \n299: ## 6. RELATED RESOURCES\n300: \n301: - [security-testing-and-exemptions.md](./security-testing-and-exemptions.md) - Security, testing, async and exemption tiers","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md, lines 1-301\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Quality Standards\n3: description: Module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns for JavaScript files.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript quality standards\"\n6:   - \"javascript module organization\"\n7:   - \"commonjs export pattern\"\n8:   - \"javascript error handling standards\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.3.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Quality Standards\n15: \n16: Reach for this file when a JavaScript module boundary, error path or security rule needs a decision that survives review.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Establishes module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns that all JavaScript code must follow.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing new JavaScript modules\n29: - Reviewing code for quality compliance\n30: - Setting up error handling and security patterns\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. MODULE ORGANIZATION\n35: \n36: ### Module-System Boundary\n37: \n38: Use the module format required by the file's package boundary and loader\n39: contract. Do not force one JavaScript module style across all paths.\n40: \n41: | Surface | Module Pattern | Notes |\n42: |---------|----------------|-------|\n43: | `.js` files | Follow the nearest package `type` and loader contract | `\"type\": \"module\"` selects ESM; otherwise use the package's CommonJS boundary |\n44: | `.cjs` files | CommonJS | The extension selects CommonJS |\n45: | `.mjs` files | ESM | The verifier skips `'use strict'` enforcement for `.mjs` |\n46: | `.opencode/plugins/*.{js,mjs,ts}` | ESM default export | Required by the OpenCode plugin loader |\n47: \n48: ### CommonJS Pattern\n49: \n50: - Use CommonJS for `.cjs` files and `.js` files whose package boundary selects CommonJS.\n51: - Include the repository's required `'use strict'` directive in those files.\n52: - ESM `.js` files follow their package boundary and also keep that directive to satisfy the repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM.\n53: \n54: ```javascript\n55: 'use strict';\n56: \n57: // ... imports and implementation ...\n58: \n59: module.exports = {\n60:   // Exports here\n61: };\n62: ```\n63: \n64: **Evidence**: Current TypeScript packages are ESM/NodeNext; this CommonJS\n65: pattern applies to legacy `.js/.cjs` utility surfaces and test runners that use\n66: `require`.\n67: \n68: ### Export Pattern\n69: \n70: - Export functions using `camelCase` names.\n71: - For MCP handlers, include `snake_case` aliases for backward compatibility.\n72: - Do not apply this export shape to OpenCode plugin paths; those use `export default`.\n73: \n74: ```javascript\n75: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n76: // 5. EXPORTS\n77: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n78: \n79: module.exports = {\n80:   // Primary exports (camelCase)\n81:   loadConfig,\n82:   triggerLookup,\n83:   validateInput,\n84: \n85:   // Backward-compatible aliases (snake_case) — MCP handlers only\n86:   load_config: loadConfig,\n87:   trigger_lookup: triggerLookup,\n88:   validate_input: validateInput\n89: };\n90: ```\n91: \n92: **Evidence**: Legacy CommonJS export examples are historical; current\n93: system-spec-kit TypeScript source uses ESM `export` statements and compiles per\n94: workspace package settings.\n95: \n96: ### Barrel Exports\n97: \n98: Index files re-export from multiple modules.\n99: \n100: ```javascript\n101: // index.js\n102: 'use strict';\n103: \n104: module.exports = {\n105:   ...require('./config'),\n106:   ...require('./database'),\n107:   ...require('./search')\n108: };\n109: ```\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 3. ERROR HANDLING\n114: \n115: ### Guard Clause Pattern\n116: \n117: Validate inputs at function start. Return early on failure.\n118: \n119: ```javascript\n120: function processData(input, options) {\n121:   // Guard clauses first\n122:   if (!input || typeof input !== 'string') {\n123:     return { success: false, error: 'Invalid input: expected string' };\n124:   }\n125: \n126:   if (!options || typeof options !== 'object') {\n127:     return { success: false, error: 'Invalid options: expected object' };\n128:   }\n129: \n130:   // Main logic after guards pass\n131:   const result = transform(input, options);\n132:   return { success: true, data: result };\n133: }\n134: ```\n135: \n136: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts`\n137: \n138: ### Try-Catch Pattern\n139: \n140: Wrap async operations with specific error handling.\n141: \n142: ```javascript\n143: async function fetchData(query) {\n144:   try {\n145:     const result = await database.query(query);\n146:     return { success: true, data: result };\n147:   } catch (error) {\n148:     logger.error(`[fetchData] Query failed: ${error.message}`);\n149:     return { success: false, error: error.message };\n150:   }\n151: }\n152: ```\n153: \n154: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts`\n155: \n156: ### Custom Error Classes\n157: \n158: Extend Error for domain-specific errors.\n159: \n160: ```javascript\n161: /**\n162:  * Custom error for spec-folder operations.\n163:  */\n164: class SpecFolderError extends Error {\n165:   /**\n166:    * Create a SpecFolderError.\n167:    * @param {string} code - Error code (e.g., 'SPEC_FOLDER_OUTSIDE_ROOT')\n168:    * @param {string} message - Error description\n169:    * @param {Object} [details] - Additional details\n170:    */\n171:   constructor(code, message, details = {}) {\n172:     super(message);\n173:     this.name = 'SpecFolderError';\n174:     this.code = code;\n175:     this.details = details;\n176:     this.recoveryHint = undefined;\n177:     Object.setPrototypeOf(this, SpecFolderError.prototype);\n178:   }\n179: }\n180: \n181: module.exports = { SpecFolderError };\n182: ```\n183: \n184: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts`\n185: \n186: ### Error Response Pattern\n187: \n188: Consistent error response structure.\n189: \n190: ```javascript\n191: // Success response\n192: { success: true, data: result }\n193: \n194: // Error response\n195: { success: false, error: 'Error message', code: 'ERROR_CODE' }\n196: ```\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 4. CONSOLE LOGGING\n201: \n202: ### Bracketed Module Prefix\n203: \n204: All console output uses bracketed module identifier.\n205: \n206: ```javascript\n207: // Format: [module-name] Message\n208: console.log(`[transaction-manager] Commit completed in ${elapsedMs}ms`);\n209: console.error(`[folder-discovery] Description merge failed: ${error.message}`);\n210: console.warn(`[config] Using default value for missing key: ${key}`);\n211: ```\n212: \n213: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/storage/transaction-manager.ts`\n214: \n215: ### Log Levels\n216: \n217: | Level   | Usage                            | Example                                    |\n218: |---------|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n219: | `log`   | General information              | `[server] Request received`                |\n220: | `error` | Errors requiring attention       | `[db] Connection failed: timeout`          |\n221: | `warn`  | Potential issues, fallbacks      | `[config] Missing value, using default`    |\n222: | `debug` | Development-only verbose output  | `[search] Query plan: ${JSON.stringify()}` |\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: ## 5. JSDOC DOCUMENTATION\n227: \n228: ### Function Documentation\n229: \n230: Required for all exported functions.\n231: \n232: ```javascript\n233: /**\n234:  * Look up trigger-index entries matching a query.\n235:  *\n236:  * @param {string} query - Search query text\n237:  * @param {Object} options - Lookup configuration\n238:  * @param {number} [options.limit=10] - Maximum results to return\n239:  * @param {string} [options.specFolder] - Filter by spec folder path\n240:  * @param {string[]} [options.anchors] - Filter by anchor types\n241:  * @returns {Promise<Array<Object>>} Array of matching trigger entries\n242:  * @throws {SpecFolderError} If the spec folder cannot be resolved\n243:  *\n244:  * @example\n245:  * const results = await lookupTriggers('authentication', {\n246:  *   limit: 5,\n247:  *   specFolder: 'specs/007-auth'\n248:  * });\n249:  */\n250: async function lookupTriggers(query, options = {}) {\n251:   // implementation\n252: }\n253: ```\n254: \n255: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/lookup-trigger-index.mjs`\n256: \n257: ### Type Annotations\n258: \n259: Common JSDoc type patterns.\n260: \n261: ```javascript\n262: /**\n263:  * @param {string} name - Required string parameter\n264:  * @param {number} [count=10] - Optional with default\n265:  * @param {Object} config - Object parameter\n266:  * @param {string} config.path - Required property\n267:  * @param {boolean} [config.verbose] - Optional property\n268:  * @param {string|null} value - Union type\n269:  * @param {Array<string>} items - Typed array\n270:  * @param {Promise<Object>} result - Promise type\n271:  * @param {Function} callback - Function type\n272:  */\n273: ```\n274: \n275: ### Class Documentation\n276: \n277: ```javascript\n278: /**\n279:  * Manages vector-based semantic search index.\n280:  *\n281:  * @class\n282:  * @example\n283:  * const index = new VectorIndex(dbPath);\n284:  * await index.initialize();\n285:  */\n286: class VectorIndex {\n287:   /**\n288:    * Create a VectorIndex instance.\n289:    * @param {string} dbPath - Path to SQLite database\n290:    */\n291:   constructor(dbPath) {\n292:     this.dbPath = dbPath;\n293:   }\n294: }\n295: ```\n296: \n297: ---\n298: \n299: ## 6. RELATED RESOURCES\n300: \n301: - [security-testing-and-exemptions.md](./security-testing-and-exemptions.md) - Security, testing, async and exemption tiers"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794559706463,"end":1794559706900}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794559706903,"sessionID":"ses_e2be9abf0ffea6S3OJ4PZfZtpb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d41803c3001b2fusSkznezDo2","sessionID":"ses_e2be9abf0ffea6S3OJ4PZfZtpb","messageID":"msg_1d417f403001E0xmq4j4fUCfLS","type":"tool","id":"call_5702bc0a8c69491db48c7154","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md, lines 1-467\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Style Guide\n3: description: Formatting standards and naming conventions for JavaScript files in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript style guide\"\n6:   - \"javascript file header format\"\n7:   - \"use strict directive\"\n8:   - \"javascript section organization\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.4.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Style Guide\n15: \n16: Open this when you are drafting or reviewing a JavaScript file and need the header, section order, naming rule or formatting detail that settles the point.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Defines consistent styling rules for JavaScript files to ensure readability, maintainability, and alignment across all OpenCode JavaScript code.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: Use when writing, reviewing, or resolving style questions for JavaScript files.\n29: \n30: ### Use Cases\n31: \n32: - Writing new JavaScript files\n33: - Reviewing JavaScript code for consistency\n34: - Resolving style disagreements in code review\n35: \n36: ---\n37: \n38: ## 2. FILE HEADER FORMAT\n39: \n40: All JavaScript files MUST begin with a module header identifying the module,\n41: using the divider form below. Module syntax follows the nearest package\n42: boundary and loader contract: `.cjs` is CommonJS, `.mjs` is ESM, and `.js`\n43: follows the nearest package's `type`. OpenCode plugin paths use their ESM\n44: loader contract.\n45: \n46: ### Template\n47: \n48: ```javascript\n49: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n50: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n51: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ### Requirements\n55: \n56: - Divider width: 67 box-drawing characters after `// `\n57: - Module name: after `MODULE:` on the middle line\n58: - A shebang line may precede the header in an executable script\n59: - Immediately followed by `'use strict';` for `.js/.cjs` files, as required by\n60:   this repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM `.js` files\n61: - `.mjs` files do not require `'use strict'`; ESM `.js` plugin files follow the\n62:   `.js` requirement above\n63: - The older 78-character `╔═╗` box is accepted where it already exists; do not\n64:   introduce it in new files\n65: \n66: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs:1-4`,\n67: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs:1-4`\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 3. USE STRICT DIRECTIVE\n72: \n73: Every `.js` and `.cjs` JavaScript file MUST include the strict mode directive.\n74: This repository convention also applies to ESM `.js` files, where it is\n75: redundant because modules are strict by definition. Do not add it to `.mjs`\n76: files; the alignment verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` enforcement.\n77: \n78: ```javascript\n79: 'use strict';\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Placement**: Immediately after file header, before any other code.\n83: \n84: **Evidence**: `verify_alignment_drift.py` skips `.mjs`; CommonJS-style JS\n85: helpers still use `'use strict'`.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 4. SECTION ORGANIZATION\n90: \n91: Large files are organized using numbered section dividers.\n92: \n93: ### Section Divider Template\n94: \n95: ```javascript\n96: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: // 1. [SECTION NAME]\n98: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n99: ```\n100: \n101: ### Standard Section Order\n102: \n103: | Order | Section Name     | Purpose                           |\n104: |-------|------------------|-----------------------------------|\n105: | 1     | IMPORTS          | Module dependencies               |\n106: | 2     | CONSTANTS        | Configuration values, magic numbers |\n107: | 3     | HELPERS          | Internal utility functions        |\n108: | 4     | CORE LOGIC       | Main implementation               |\n109: | 5     | EXPORTS          | Module public interface           |\n110: \n111: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts`\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 5. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n116: \n117: ### Function Names\n118: \n119: **Style**: `camelCase`\n120: \n121: ```javascript\n122: // CORRECT\n123: function loadConfig(path) { }\n124: function memorySearch(query, options) { }\n125: function validateInput(data) { }\n126: \n127: // INCORRECT\n128: function load_config(path) { }     // snake_case\n129: function LoadConfig(path) { }      // PascalCase\n130: function load-config(path) { }     // kebab-case (invalid syntax)\n131: ```\n132: \n133: **Evidence**: MDN Web Docs, Airbnb Style Guide, Node.js core APIs\n134: \n135: ### Constant Names\n136: \n137: **Style**: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`\n138: \n139: ```javascript\n140: // CORRECT\n141: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n142: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n143: const SQLITE_BUSY_TIMEOUT = 30000;\n144: \n145: // INCORRECT\n146: const maxRetries = 3;       // camelCase\n147: const max_retries = 3;      // snake_case (reserved for variables)\n148: ```\n149: \n150: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/core/config.ts`\n151: \n152: ### Class Names\n153: \n154: **Style**: `PascalCase`\n155: \n156: ```javascript\n157: // CORRECT\n158: class SpecFolderError extends Error { }\n159: class ConfigLoader { }\n160: class TriggerIndex { }\n161: \n162: // INCORRECT\n163: class specFolderError { }   // camelCase\n164: class spec_folder_error { } // snake_case\n165: ```\n166: \n167: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts`\n168: \n169: ### Variable Names\n170: \n171: | Scope        | Style        | Example                          |\n172: |--------------|--------------|----------------------------------|\n173: | Local        | `camelCase`  | `const searchResults = []`       |\n174: | Module-level | `camelCase`  | `const dbPath = '...'`           |\n175: | Parameters   | `camelCase`  | `function search(query, maxResults)` |\n176: \n177: ### Boolean Names\n178: \n179: **Style**: `camelCase` with `is`/`has`/`can`/`should` prefix\n180: \n181: ```javascript\n182: // CORRECT\n183: const isValid = true;\n184: const hasResults = items.length > 0;\n185: const canProceed = !isBlocked;\n186: const shouldRetry = attempts < MAX_RETRIES;\n187: \n188: // INCORRECT\n189: const is_valid = true;       // snake_case\n190: const valid = true;          // ambiguous\n191: ```\n192: \n193: ### Naming Summary Table\n194: \n195: | Element          | Convention         | Example                |\n196: |------------------|--------------------|------------------------|\n197: | Functions        | `camelCase`        | `loadConfig`           |\n198: | Constants        | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_RETRIES`          |\n199: | Classes          | `PascalCase`       | `MemoryError`          |\n200: | Local variables  | `camelCase`        | `searchResults`        |\n201: | Module variables | `camelCase`        | `dbConnection`         |\n202: | Parameters       | `camelCase`        | `queryText`            |\n203: | Booleans         | `is`/`has`/`can`   | `isValid`, `hasItems`  |\n204: \n205: ---\n206: \n207: ## 6. FORMATTING RULES\n208: \n209: ### Indentation\n210: \n211: - **Size**: 2 spaces\n212: - **Tabs**: Never use tabs\n213: \n214: ```javascript\n215: // CORRECT (2 spaces)\n216: function example() {\n217:   if (condition) {\n218:     return value;\n219:   }\n220: }\n221: \n222: // INCORRECT (4 spaces or tabs)\n223: function example() {\n224:     if (condition) {\n225:         return value;\n226:     }\n227: }\n228: ```\n229: \n230: ### Braces\n231: \n232: **Style**: K&R (opening brace on same line)\n233: \n234: ```javascript\n235: // CORRECT (K&R style)\n236: if (condition) {\n237:   // code\n238: } else {\n239:   // code\n240: }\n241: \n242: function example() {\n243:   // code\n244: }\n245: \n246: // INCORRECT (Allman style)\n247: if (condition)\n248: {\n249:   // code\n250: }\n251: ```\n252: \n253: ### Semicolons\n254: \n255: **Rule**: Always use semicolons\n256: \n257: ```javascript\n258: // CORRECT\n259: const value = 42;\n260: return result;\n261: \n262: // INCORRECT (ASI-dependent)\n263: const value = 42\n264: return result\n265: ```\n266: \n267: ### Quotes\n268: \n269: **Rule**: Single quotes for strings\n270: \n271: ```javascript\n272: // CORRECT\n273: const message = 'Hello world';\n274: const template = `Value: ${value}`;  // Template literals OK\n275: \n276: // INCORRECT\n277: const message = \"Hello world\";  // Double quotes\n278: ```\n279: \n280: ### Line Length\n281: \n282: - **Soft limit**: roughly 100 to 120 characters, per the shared formatting rule\n283: - **Preferred**: 80 characters\n284: - **Exception**: URLs and long strings\n285: \n286: ---\n287: \n288: ## 7. COMMENTING RULES\n289: \n290: ### Principles\n291: \n292: 1. **Quantity limit (Skilled surface setting):** Maximum 3 comments per 10 lines of code, this surface's budget for the shared comment-density rule (see [naming and commenting](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md))\n293: 2. **Focus on purposeful semantics:** Explain WHY something is done, not WHAT it does\n294: 3. **Focus on WHY, not WHAT:** Explain intent, constraints, reasoning\n295: 4. **No commented-out code:** Delete unused code (git preserves history)\n296: \n297: ### Comment Examples\n298: \n299: Use plain comments that explain reasoning:\n300: \n301: - `// Reasoning for non-obvious choice`\n302: - `// Precondition/invariant check`\n303: - `// State that must always hold`\n304: - `// SEC: security note tied to a stable standard (CWE-###)`\n305: - `// Safety, performance, or reliability constraint`\n306: \n307: ### Capitalization\n308: \n309: All comment text MUST start with a capital letter:\n310: \n311: ```javascript\n312: // Reverse order preserves dependency chain     // correct\n313: // reverse order preserves dependency chain     // wrong\n314: ```\n315: \n316: **Exceptions**: `eslint-disable` directives, `@ts-` annotations, and inline code references.\n317: \n318: ### Reference Comments\n319: \n320: Use a bracketed module prefix for inline comments:\n321: \n322: ```javascript\n323: // [module-name] Description of what this does\n324: ```\n325: \n326: Cite only durable references, never a spec-folder-internal id. Comments must not embed a spec folder/number, task/checklist/requirement id (`T###`, `CHK-###`, `REQ-###`), feature-catalog entry, ADR id, or ticket id; see [`../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md) §4 and [`../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) §4 \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\".\n327: \n328: ```javascript\n329: // GOOD — durable WHY, no ephemeral id\n330: // Causal Memory Graph handlers: drift-why, causal-link, causal-stats, causal-unlink\n331: // Recover pending writes on startup so a crash cannot lose data\n332: // SEC: sanitize input to prevent stored XSS (CWE-79)\n333: ```\n334: \n335: ### Function Purpose Comments\n336: \n337: Single line above function describing intent:\n338: \n339: ```javascript\n340: // Load configuration from YAML file, merging with defaults\n341: // Returns validated config object or throws on invalid schema\n342: function loadConfig(configPath) { }\n343: \n344: // Calculate weighted memory score using decay function\n345: // Higher scores indicate more recent and relevant memories\n346: function calculateMemoryScore(memory, currentTime) { }\n347: ```\n348: \n349: ### Inline Comments (WHY, Not WHAT)\n350: \n351: **Good examples (explain reasoning):**\n352: \n353: ```javascript\n354: // Skip if already initialized to prevent double-binding\n355: if (window[INIT_FLAG]) return;\n356: \n357: // Sort by recency so newest memories surface first\n358: results.sort((a, b) => b.timestamp - a.timestamp);\n359: \n360: // Add timeout to prevent hang on unresponsive database\n361: const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>\n362:   setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timeout')), 30000)\n363: );\n364: \n365: // Close DB connection on all control paths\n366: try {\n367:   await processBatch(items);\n368: } finally {\n369:   db.close();\n370: }\n371: ```\n372: \n373: **Bad examples (narrate implementation):**\n374: \n375: ```javascript\n376: // Set the value to 42\n377: const value = 42;\n378: \n379: // Loop through items\n380: for (const item of items) { }\n381: \n382: // Check if result exists\n383: if (result) { }\n384: \n385: // Add item to array\n386: items.push(item);\n387: ```\n388: \n389: ### Debug Logging Pattern\n390: \n391: ```javascript\n392: // Conditional logging for debug mode\n393: const DEBUG = false;\n394: const LOG_PREFIX = '[ModuleName]';\n395: \n396: function log(...args) {\n397:   if (DEBUG) {\n398:     console.log(LOG_PREFIX, ...args);\n399:   }\n400: }\n401: \n402: // Usage\n403: log('Initialized with config:', config);\n404: log('Processing batch:', batch.length, 'items');\n405: ```\n406: \n407: ### JSDoc Comments\n408: \n409: Required for all exported functions. See `quality-standards.md` for full JSDoc format.\n410: \n411: ```javascript\n412: /**\n413:  * Brief description of function purpose.\n414:  *\n415:  * @param {string} query - Search query text\n416:  * @param {Object} options - Search options\n417:  * @param {number} options.limit - Maximum results to return\n418:  * @returns {Array} Array of search results\n419:  */\n420: function search(query, options = {}) {\n421:   // implementation\n422: }\n423: ```\n424: \n425: ### TODO Comments\n426: \n427: Format for tracking incomplete work.\n428: \n429: ```javascript\n430: // TODO: [description of work needed]\n431: // FIXME: [description of bug to fix]\n432: ```\n433: \n434: ---\n435: \n436: ## 8. IMPORT ORDER\n437: \n438: Organize imports in three groups with blank lines between.\n439: \n440: ```javascript\n441: 'use strict';\n442: \n443: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n444: // 1. IMPORTS\n445: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n446: \n447: // Node.js built-ins\n448: const fs = require('fs');\n449: const path = require('path');\n450: \n451: // Third-party modules\n452: const yaml = require('js-yaml');\n453: const sqlite = require('better-sqlite3');\n454: \n455: // Local modules\n456: const { loadConfig } = require('./config');\n457: const logger = require('../utils/logger');\n458: ```\n459: \n460: **Evidence**: `mcp-server/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts:1-26`\n461: \n462: ---\n463: \n464: ## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n465: \n466: - [quality-standards.md](quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md) - Error handling, JSDoc, security patterns\n467: - [quick-reference.md](./quick-reference.md) - Copy-paste templates and cheat sheets","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md, lines 1-467\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Style Guide\n3: description: Formatting standards and naming conventions for JavaScript files in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript style guide\"\n6:   - \"javascript file header format\"\n7:   - \"use strict directive\"\n8:   - \"javascript section organization\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.4.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Style Guide\n15: \n16: Open this when you are drafting or reviewing a JavaScript file and need the header, section order, naming rule or formatting detail that settles the point.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Defines consistent styling rules for JavaScript files to ensure readability, maintainability, and alignment across all OpenCode JavaScript code.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: Use when writing, reviewing, or resolving style questions for JavaScript files.\n29: \n30: ### Use Cases\n31: \n32: - Writing new JavaScript files\n33: - Reviewing JavaScript code for consistency\n34: - Resolving style disagreements in code review\n35: \n36: ---\n37: \n38: ## 2. FILE HEADER FORMAT\n39: \n40: All JavaScript files MUST begin with a module header identifying the module,\n41: using the divider form below. Module syntax follows the nearest package\n42: boundary and loader contract: `.cjs` is CommonJS, `.mjs` is ESM, and `.js`\n43: follows the nearest package's `type`. OpenCode plugin paths use their ESM\n44: loader contract.\n45: \n46: ### Template\n47: \n48: ```javascript\n49: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n50: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n51: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ### Requirements\n55: \n56: - Divider width: 67 box-drawing characters after `// `\n57: - Module name: after `MODULE:` on the middle line\n58: - A shebang line may precede the header in an executable script\n59: - Immediately followed by `'use strict';` for `.js/.cjs` files, as required by\n60:   this repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM `.js` files\n61: - `.mjs` files do not require `'use strict'`; ESM `.js` plugin files follow the\n62:   `.js` requirement above\n63: - The older 78-character `╔═╗` box is accepted where it already exists; do not\n64:   introduce it in new files\n65: \n66: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs:1-4`,\n67: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs:1-4`\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 3. USE STRICT DIRECTIVE\n72: \n73: Every `.js` and `.cjs` JavaScript file MUST include the strict mode directive.\n74: This repository convention also applies to ESM `.js` files, where it is\n75: redundant because modules are strict by definition. Do not add it to `.mjs`\n76: files; the alignment verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` enforcement.\n77: \n78: ```javascript\n79: 'use strict';\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Placement**: Immediately after file header, before any other code.\n83: \n84: **Evidence**: `verify_alignment_drift.py` skips `.mjs`; CommonJS-style JS\n85: helpers still use `'use strict'`.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 4. SECTION ORGANIZATION\n90: \n91: Large files are organized using numbered section dividers.\n92: \n93: ### Section Divider Template\n94: \n95: ```javascript\n96: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: // 1. [SECTION NAME]\n98: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n99: ```\n100: \n101: ### Standard Section Order\n102: \n103: | Order | Section Name     | Purpose                           |\n104: |-------|------------------|-----------------------------------|\n105: | 1     | IMPORTS          | Module dependencies               |\n106: | 2     | CONSTANTS        | Configuration values, magic numbers |\n107: | 3     | HELPERS          | Internal utility functions        |\n108: | 4     | CORE LOGIC       | Main implementation               |\n109: | 5     | EXPORTS          | Module public interface           |\n110: \n111: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts`\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 5. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n116: \n117: ### Function Names\n118: \n119: **Style**: `camelCase`\n120: \n121: ```javascript\n122: // CORRECT\n123: function loadConfig(path) { }\n124: function memorySearch(query, options) { }\n125: function validateInput(data) { }\n126: \n127: // INCORRECT\n128: function load_config(path) { }     // snake_case\n129: function LoadConfig(path) { }      // PascalCase\n130: function load-config(path) { }     // kebab-case (invalid syntax)\n131: ```\n132: \n133: **Evidence**: MDN Web Docs, Airbnb Style Guide, Node.js core APIs\n134: \n135: ### Constant Names\n136: \n137: **Style**: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`\n138: \n139: ```javascript\n140: // CORRECT\n141: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n142: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n143: const SQLITE_BUSY_TIMEOUT = 30000;\n144: \n145: // INCORRECT\n146: const maxRetries = 3;       // camelCase\n147: const max_retries = 3;      // snake_case (reserved for variables)\n148: ```\n149: \n150: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/core/config.ts`\n151: \n152: ### Class Names\n153: \n154: **Style**: `PascalCase`\n155: \n156: ```javascript\n157: // CORRECT\n158: class SpecFolderError extends Error { }\n159: class ConfigLoader { }\n160: class TriggerIndex { }\n161: \n162: // INCORRECT\n163: class specFolderError { }   // camelCase\n164: class spec_folder_error { } // snake_case\n165: ```\n166: \n167: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts`\n168: \n169: ### Variable Names\n170: \n171: | Scope        | Style        | Example                          |\n172: |--------------|--------------|----------------------------------|\n173: | Local        | `camelCase`  | `const searchResults = []`       |\n174: | Module-level | `camelCase`  | `const dbPath = '...'`           |\n175: | Parameters   | `camelCase`  | `function search(query, maxResults)` |\n176: \n177: ### Boolean Names\n178: \n179: **Style**: `camelCase` with `is`/`has`/`can`/`should` prefix\n180: \n181: ```javascript\n182: // CORRECT\n183: const isValid = true;\n184: const hasResults = items.length > 0;\n185: const canProceed = !isBlocked;\n186: const shouldRetry = attempts < MAX_RETRIES;\n187: \n188: // INCORRECT\n189: const is_valid = true;       // snake_case\n190: const valid = true;          // ambiguous\n191: ```\n192: \n193: ### Naming Summary Table\n194: \n195: | Element          | Convention         | Example                |\n196: |------------------|--------------------|------------------------|\n197: | Functions        | `camelCase`        | `loadConfig`           |\n198: | Constants        | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_RETRIES`          |\n199: | Classes          | `PascalCase`       | `MemoryError`          |\n200: | Local variables  | `camelCase`        | `searchResults`        |\n201: | Module variables | `camelCase`        | `dbConnection`         |\n202: | Parameters       | `camelCase`        | `queryText`            |\n203: | Booleans         | `is`/`has`/`can`   | `isValid`, `hasItems`  |\n204: \n205: ---\n206: \n207: ## 6. FORMATTING RULES\n208: \n209: ### Indentation\n210: \n211: - **Size**: 2 spaces\n212: - **Tabs**: Never use tabs\n213: \n214: ```javascript\n215: // CORRECT (2 spaces)\n216: function example() {\n217:   if (condition) {\n218:     return value;\n219:   }\n220: }\n221: \n222: // INCORRECT (4 spaces or tabs)\n223: function example() {\n224:     if (condition) {\n225:         return value;\n226:     }\n227: }\n228: ```\n229: \n230: ### Braces\n231: \n232: **Style**: K&R (opening brace on same line)\n233: \n234: ```javascript\n235: // CORRECT (K&R style)\n236: if (condition) {\n237:   // code\n238: } else {\n239:   // code\n240: }\n241: \n242: function example() {\n243:   // code\n244: }\n245: \n246: // INCORRECT (Allman style)\n247: if (condition)\n248: {\n249:   // code\n250: }\n251: ```\n252: \n253: ### Semicolons\n254: \n255: **Rule**: Always use semicolons\n256: \n257: ```javascript\n258: // CORRECT\n259: const value = 42;\n260: return result;\n261: \n262: // INCORRECT (ASI-dependent)\n263: const value = 42\n264: return result\n265: ```\n266: \n267: ### Quotes\n268: \n269: **Rule**: Single quotes for strings\n270: \n271: ```javascript\n272: // CORRECT\n273: const message = 'Hello world';\n274: const template = `Value: ${value}`;  // Template literals OK\n275: \n276: // INCORRECT\n277: const message = \"Hello world\";  // Double quotes\n278: ```\n279: \n280: ### Line Length\n281: \n282: - **Soft limit**: roughly 100 to 120 characters, per the shared formatting rule\n283: - **Preferred**: 80 characters\n284: - **Exception**: URLs and long strings\n285: \n286: ---\n287: \n288: ## 7. COMMENTING RULES\n289: \n290: ### Principles\n291: \n292: 1. **Quantity limit (Skilled surface setting):** Maximum 3 comments per 10 lines of code, this surface's budget for the shared comment-density rule (see [naming and commenting](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md))\n293: 2. **Focus on purposeful semantics:** Explain WHY something is done, not WHAT it does\n294: 3. **Focus on WHY, not WHAT:** Explain intent, constraints, reasoning\n295: 4. **No commented-out code:** Delete unused code (git preserves history)\n296: \n297: ### Comment Examples\n298: \n299: Use plain comments that explain reasoning:\n300: \n301: - `// Reasoning for non-obvious choice`\n302: - `// Precondition/invariant check`\n303: - `// State that must always hold`\n304: - `// SEC: security note tied to a stable standard (CWE-###)`\n305: - `// Safety, performance, or reliability constraint`\n306: \n307: ### Capitalization\n308: \n309: All comment text MUST start with a capital letter:\n310: \n311: ```javascript\n312: // Reverse order preserves dependency chain     // correct\n313: // reverse order preserves dependency chain     // wrong\n314: ```\n315: \n316: **Exceptions**: `eslint-disable` directives, `@ts-` annotations, and inline code references.\n317: \n318: ### Reference Comments\n319: \n320: Use a bracketed module prefix for inline comments:\n321: \n322: ```javascript\n323: // [module-name] Description of what this does\n324: ```\n325: \n326: Cite only durable references, never a spec-folder-internal id. Comments must not embed a spec folder/number, task/checklist/requirement id (`T###`, `CHK-###`, `REQ-###`), feature-catalog entry, ADR id, or ticket id; see [`../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md) §4 and [`../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) §4 \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\".\n327: \n328: ```javascript\n329: // GOOD — durable WHY, no ephemeral id\n330: // Causal Memory Graph handlers: drift-why, causal-link, causal-stats, causal-unlink\n331: // Recover pending writes on startup so a crash cannot lose data\n332: // SEC: sanitize input to prevent stored XSS (CWE-79)\n333: ```\n334: \n335: ### Function Purpose Comments\n336: \n337: Single line above function describing intent:\n338: \n339: ```javascript\n340: // Load configuration from YAML file, merging with defaults\n341: // Returns validated config object or throws on invalid schema\n342: function loadConfig(configPath) { }\n343: \n344: // Calculate weighted memory score using decay function\n345: // Higher scores indicate more recent and relevant memories\n346: function calculateMemoryScore(memory, currentTime) { }\n347: ```\n348: \n349: ### Inline Comments (WHY, Not WHAT)\n350: \n351: **Good examples (explain reasoning):**\n352: \n353: ```javascript\n354: // Skip if already initialized to prevent double-binding\n355: if (window[INIT_FLAG]) return;\n356: \n357: // Sort by recency so newest memories surface first\n358: results.sort((a, b) => b.timestamp - a.timestamp);\n359: \n360: // Add timeout to prevent hang on unresponsive database\n361: const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>\n362:   setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timeout')), 30000)\n363: );\n364: \n365: // Close DB connection on all control paths\n366: try {\n367:   await processBatch(items);\n368: } finally {\n369:   db.close();\n370: }\n371: ```\n372: \n373: **Bad examples (narrate implementation):**\n374: \n375: ```javascript\n376: // Set the value to 42\n377: const value = 42;\n378: \n379: // Loop through items\n380: for (const item of items) { }\n381: \n382: // Check if result exists\n383: if (result) { }\n384: \n385: // Add item to array\n386: items.push(item);\n387: ```\n388: \n389: ### Debug Logging Pattern\n390: \n391: ```javascript\n392: // Conditional logging for debug mode\n393: const DEBUG = false;\n394: const LOG_PREFIX = '[ModuleName]';\n395: \n396: function log(...args) {\n397:   if (DEBUG) {\n398:     console.log(LOG_PREFIX, ...args);\n399:   }\n400: }\n401: \n402: // Usage\n403: log('Initialized with config:', config);\n404: log('Processing batch:', batch.length, 'items');\n405: ```\n406: \n407: ### JSDoc Comments\n408: \n409: Required for all exported functions. See `quality-standards.md` for full JSDoc format.\n410: \n411: ```javascript\n412: /**\n413:  * Brief description of function purpose.\n414:  *\n415:  * @param {string} query - Search query text\n416:  * @param {Object} options - Search options\n417:  * @param {number} options.limit - Maximum results to return\n418:  * @returns {Array} Array of search results\n419:  */\n420: function search(query, options = {}) {\n421:   // implementation\n422: }\n423: ```\n424: \n425: ### TODO Comments\n426: \n427: Format for tracking incomplete work.\n428: \n429: ```javascript\n430: // TODO: [description of work needed]\n431: // FIXME: [description of bug to fix]\n432: ```\n433: \n434: ---\n435: \n436: ## 8. IMPORT ORDER\n437: \n438: Organize imports in three groups with blank lines between.\n439: \n440: ```javascript\n441: 'use strict';\n442: \n443: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n444: // 1. IMPORTS\n445: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n446: \n447: // Node.js built-ins\n448: const fs = require('fs');\n449: const path = require('path');\n450: \n451: // Third-party modules\n452: const yaml = require('js-yaml');\n453: const sqlite = require('better-sqlite3');\n454: \n455: // Local modules\n456: const { loadConfig } = require('./config');\n457: const logger = require('../utils/logger');\n458: ```\n459: \n460: **Evidence**: `mcp-server/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts:1-26`\n461: \n462: ---\n463: \n464: ## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n465: \n466: - [quality-standards.md](quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md) - Error handling, JSDoc, security patterns\n467: - [quick-reference.md](./quick-reference.md) - Copy-paste templates and cheat sheets"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794559706051,"end":1794559706903}}}}
